Imperaterm
==========

Imperaterm is a lightning fast terminal emulator for CGTK. It is minimal, but
does have some QoL features: tabs, a scrollback, selections and a few settings.
What it shows is drawn with OpenGL.

It is an xterm to the programs that run in it: TERM is xterm-256color,
and 24 bit colour, the alternate screen, bracketed paste, mouse
reporting, focus reporting and synchronized output are there. The
terminal's logic derives from st.


What it needs
-------------

  CGTK 1.4, built with its OpenGL widget (gtkgl)
  FreeType and fontconfig, which CGTK needs as well
  OpenGL 3.1 or OpenGL ES 3.0 when it runs
  gettext, for its translations: autoreconf wants autopoint, and make
    wants msgfmt

No GL library is linked. Imperaterm asks for an OpenGL 3.1 context, and
for an OpenGL ES 3.0 one where there is no such thing. To have OpenGL ES
where both are there, as on a board whose driver does it better, set
IMPERATERM_GLES in the environment. Help > About says which one is in
use.


Building
--------

  autoreconf -vif
  ./configure
  make
  make check
  make install

make check runs the terminal without a window: sequences go in, and the
cells and replies that come out are compared with what they should be.
tests/bench, built with it, measures how fast the terminal takes text.

make install puts the icon where a desktop looks for one: the drawing in
the hicolor theme's scalable directory, PNGs of it from 16 to 256 pixels
beside that, and an XPM in pixmaps. The PNGs and the XPM are in icons/
already, made from the one SVG there by icons/render.sh, which needs
rsvg-convert and ImageMagick and is not part of the build.

./configure --disable-nls builds it in English only.


Keys
----

  Ctrl+Shift+T          new tab, in the directory of the current one
  Ctrl+Shift+N          new window
  Ctrl+Shift+W          close the tab
  Ctrl+Shift+Q          close the window
  Ctrl+Page Up, Down    previous and next tab
  Ctrl+Shift+Page Up,   move the tab left and right
    Down
  Ctrl+Shift+C          copy the selection to the clipboard
  Ctrl+Shift+V          paste the clipboard
  Shift+Insert          paste the primary selection
  Shift+Page Up, Down   scroll back and forth by a page
  Shift+Home, End       to the top of the scrollback and to its end
  Ctrl++, Ctrl+-,       larger and smaller text, and the size as set
    Ctrl+0

Alt and a key send Escape and the key.


Tabs
----

A tab says who is at the terminal and where, as a prompt does:

  user@host: directory

with ~ for home. Of a long directory the tab shows the end, after three
dots; the window's title has all of it. A program that sets a title, as
some shells do at each prompt, has the tab called that. How the tab
knows who and where is explained under Who and where, below.

The right button on a tab has a menu, and Rename Tab in it gives the tab
a custom name. The name lasts until the tab is closed and is saved nowhere.
An empty name gives the tab back its own.

A tab dragged along the bar with the left button changes places with
those it passes. The Tabs menu and its keys move it too.

Dragged off the bar, the tab is carried. Dropped on another window it
goes to that window, at the place in its bar where it is dropped or at
the end if it is dropped on the terminal. Dropped where there is no
window of Imperaterm it has a new window of its own. Dropped back on the
window it came from it stays where it was, and with X so it does when
Escape is pressed on the way. A tab goes between the windows of one
Imperaterm, and the windows on a display are all of one: see One
Imperaterm below.

The tabs are at the top of the window, under the menubar. Tabs at the
bottom in Edit > Preferences puts them under the terminal, in every
window.


The mouse
---------

Dragging with the left button selects, a double click a word and a
triple click a line. Shift and a click move the end of the selection.
With Control held the selection is a block. What is selected is the
primary selection, which the middle button pastes. The right button has
a menu.

The wheel scrolls the scrollback. In a program that uses the alternate
screen, such as less, it sends the arrow keys.

A program that asks for the mouse gets it. Hold Shift to select all the
same.


Settings
--------

Edit > Preferences has the font, its size, the colours, the cursor, the
scrollback, the bell, where the tabs are, the menubar and the scrollbar.
A change there is in effect as it is made, and there is nothing to
confirm. A font typed in is taken once it is the name of one there is,
or at Enter. The settings are kept in

  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/imperaterm/imperatermrc

which is ~/.config/imperaterm/imperatermrc unless that is set. The
number of scrollback lines is for the tabs opened after it is changed.
The size of a new window is there too, as columns and rows, though not
in the dialog.

View > Menubar and View > Scrollbar show and hide those, in every
window, and are kept too. Both are in the menu of the right button as
well, which is a way back to a hidden menubar, and Preferences has
both.

The first two sets of colours, Default (Dark) and Default (Light), are
unique to Imperaterm, with Default (Dark) as the default. The other sets
are well known by the names they have here.

The last set, Custom, is the user's own, and starts as a copy of the
first. The Custom... button beside the list chooses it and opens its
editor. Each colour there has a swatch, which opens a colour selector,
and its value as rrggbb, which can be typed. Copy from makes Custom a
copy of another set to start from. In the settings file Custom is
colour0 to colour15, foreground, background and cursorcolour, each as
#rrggbb.


Running a command
-----------------

  imperaterm -e command arguments

runs the command in place of the shell. The window closes when it ends.


One Imperaterm
--------------

All the windows on a display are one Imperaterm. An Imperaterm that is
started while another runs, from a key, a menu or a command line, hands
that one what it was to run and has a window of it. The command or the
shell runs in the directory and with the environment of the Imperaterm
that was started, and that Imperaterm waits until the tab that came of
it is gone, so that what waits for a terminal to close still does.

This is what lets a tab be carried from any window to any other. A new
window comes up sooner, and a change in Preferences is every window's
at once. The price is that the windows stand and fall together.

  imperaterm --separate

is an Imperaterm of its own: it joins none and none joins it. So is one
that is given an option of GTK's, such as --display.

A display is what is drawn to, the X server or the Wayland compositor,
with however many monitors it has. The Imperaterms meet at a socket in
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, which must be the user's alone, as it is meant to be;
where it is not set, or is open to others, each Imperaterm is its own.
One that gets no answer within five seconds runs on its own too.


Who and where
-------------

The name on a tab, user@host: directory, comes from the shell. Imperaterm
cannot work it out by itself. Each system has its own way of looking
into another program, and even where there is one, it gives the wrong
answer after su or ssh and no answer at all for root. So the shell tells
the terminal at each prompt, with two escape sequences that other
terminals understand as well:

  OSC 7     file://host/directory
  OSC 1337  RemoteHost=user@host

Until the shell says anything, the tab shows the user Imperaterm runs
as, this host, and the directory the tab started in. If the shell never
says anything, the tab stays that way. A new tab opens in the directory
the current tab last reported, if that is on this host. If it cannot go
there, it opens at home.

The shell script imperaterm.sh does the telling. It works in sh, ash,
dash, ksh, bash and zsh, and is installed as share/imperaterm/imperaterm.sh.
It only speaks in a shell someone is typing at, and only when TERM begins
with xterm, so it does no harm anywhere else. Which shells run it:

  The shell Imperaterm starts runs it with no setup. Imperaterm sets
  PROMPT_COMMAND for bash, and ENV for sh, ash, dash and ksh, unless they
  are set already. A shell that keeps that environment, as after su,
  runs it too.

  Login shells, such as those of su -, sudo -i and ssh, run it if it is
  in /etc/profile.d. make install puts it in profile.d under sysconfdir,
  which is /etc only if Imperaterm was configured with --sysconfdir=/etc.
  Otherwise, copy or link it there yourself.

  Any other shell, such as that of sudo -s or doas -s, reads only its own
  start-up files. For bash, add this line to the system's bashrc or to
  ~/.bashrc. For zsh, add it to ~/.zshrc:

    . /etc/profile.d/imperaterm.sh

  If your .bashrc sets PROMPT_COMMAND itself, put the line after that.
  An ash started this way reads no start-up file, so it cannot tell.

  fish cannot read the script. Put this in config.fish instead:

    function __imperaterm_report --on-event fish_prompt
        printf '\e]7;file://%s%s\a\e]1337;RemoteHost=%s@%s\a' $hostname \
            (string escape --style=url -- $PWD) $USER $hostname
    end

To see the user, host and directory on another machine you reach with
ssh, put the script in /etc/profile.d on that machine too.


How it draws
------------

A terminal is a small texture with two texels for each cell: its
colours, where its glyph is in the atlas, and whether it is underlined
or struck through. One triangle covers the window. For each pixel the
fragment shader finds the cell, then the glyph's pixel. Nothing is drawn
twice, nothing is blended and no integer is divided, which suits a
modest GPU.

Nothing is drawn when nothing has changed. What a program writes is
drawn when it pauses, three milliseconds after it last wrote, so that a
change it makes to the screen in many small writes is seen whole and not
half made. When a program writes without pause, it is read as fast as it
writes and the window is drawn once for each frame of the screen.

Line drawing and block characters are drawn by Imperaterm, not taken
from the font, so that they join. Double lines, dashes and arcs are the
font's.


What is not there
-----------------

  Combining characters: a character of no width is dropped.
  Colour emoji, and ligatures.
  The kitty keyboard and graphics protocols.
  Preedit text of an input method is not shown in the terminal; what
  the input method commits is typed.


Translations
------------

Imperaterm's text domain is imperaterm, with its catalogues in po:
Chinese (simplified), French, German, Japanese, Portuguese (Brazil),
Russian and Spanish. The desktop file carries the same languages. The
names of the sets of colours stay as they are but for Default and
Custom, and so does what the settings file says. The translations were
machine translated and have not been reviewed by native speakers.


